NNAAMMEE
       xattr - Extended attributes

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Extended  attributes  are  name:value pairs associated permanently with
       files and directories, similar to the  environment  strings  associated
       with  a  process.   An attribute may be defined or undefined.  If it is
       defined, its value may be empty or non‐empty.

       Extended attributes are extensions to the normal attributes  which  are
       associated  with  all  inodes  in  the system (i.e., the ssttaatt(2) data).
       They are often used to provide additional functionality to  a  filesys‐
       tem—for  example,  additional  security features such as Access Control
       Lists (ACLs) may be implemented using extended attributes.

       Users with search access to a file or directory may use lliissttxxaattttrr(2) to
       retrieve a list of attribute names defined for that file or directory.

       Extended attributes are accessed as  atomic  objects.   Reading  (ggeettxx‐‐
       aattttrr(2))  retrieves  the whole value of an attribute and stores it in a
       buffer.  Writing (sseettxxaattttrr(2)) replaces any previous value with the new
       value.

       Space consumed for extended attributes may be counted towards the  disk
       quotas of the file owner and file group.

   EExxtteennddeedd aattttrriibbuuttee nnaammeessppaacceess
